const e="revision-collection",t=1,o=[{name:"WJEC Biology Fieldbook",id:"fieldbook-0",parent:null},{name:"Unit 1 · Cells, systems and ecosystems",id:"fieldbook-1",parent:"fieldbook-0"},{name:"Unit 2 · Variation and regulation",id:"fieldbook-2",parent:"fieldbook-0"},{name:"Unit 3 · Enquiry and exam practice",id:"fieldbook-3",parent:"fieldbook-0"}],i=[{name:"WJEC GCSE Biology",color:"mint"}],n=JSON.parse('[{"id":"fieldbook-start","title":"WJEC GCSE Biology: Revision Fieldbook","body":"Start with a lesson you find hard, or take the pack quiz closed-book. Use the spider maps to move between topics, and do the mixed mini-paper once you have revised both units.\\n\\nSeparate WJEC GCSE Biology (3400QS / C00/0779/8), not Eduqas or either WJEC Double Award. Units 1 and 2 each have an 80-mark, 1 h 45 min paper worth 45%. The specification uses bold for Higher-only content; check your tier against it. ([WJEC separate GCSE Biology specification](https://www.wjec.co.uk/media/m1lm4qb5/wjec-gcse-biology-from-2016-spec.pdf))\\n\\nFor students sitting Unit 3 in January–February 2027, WJEC lists the legacy Practical Assessment. For Year 10 starters from September 2026, WJEC specifies revised Scientific Enquiry, first awarded in 2028. Ask your teacher which applies if your entry is unusual. The earlier examiner reports describe the legacy assessment, not the new format. ([WJEC Biology qualification page](https://www.wjec.co.uk/qualifications/biology-gcse/); [WJEC decision on separate sciences and Unit 3](https://www.wjec.co.uk/articles/survey-outcomes-gcse-biology-chemistry-and-physics/))\\n\\n## Unit 1\\n\\n- [[1.1 Cells, membranes and enzymes]]\\n\\n- [[1.2 Respiration and breathing]]\\n\\n- [[1.3 Digestion and nutrition]]\\n\\n- [[1.4 Heart, blood and vessels]]\\n\\n- [[1.5 Photosynthesis and plant transport]]\\n\\n- [[1.6 Ecosystems and nutrient cycles]]\\n\\n## Unit 2\\n\\n- [[2.1 Classification and biodiversity]]\\n\\n- [[2.2 Mitosis, meiosis and stem cells]]\\n\\n- [[2.3 DNA and inheritance]]\\n\\n- [[2.4 Variation and evolution]]\\n\\n- [[2.5 Response and regulation]]\\n\\n- [[2.6 Kidneys and water balance]]\\n\\n- [[2.7 Micro-organisms and applications]]\\n\\n- [[2.8 Disease, defence and treatment]]\\n\\n## Practical and exam skills\\n\\n- [[3 Scientific enquiry and data skills]]\\n\\n- [[Biology spider map: Unit 1]]\\n\\n- [[Biology spider map: Unit 2]]\\n\\n- [[Mixed mini-paper · 26 marks]]\\n\\n- [[Mixed mini-paper · Self-marking guide]]\\n\\n- [[Examiner lessons and revision plan]]\\n\\n## Sources\\n\\n- [WJEC separate GCSE Biology specification](https://www.wjec.co.uk/media/m1lm4qb5/wjec-gcse-biology-from-2016-spec.pdf)\\n\\n- [WJEC Biology qualification page](https://www.wjec.co.uk/qualifications/biology-gcse/)\\n\\n- [WJEC decision on separate sciences and Unit 3](https://www.wjec.co.uk/articles/survey-outcomes-gcse-biology-chemistry-and-physics/)\\n\\n- [WJEC Made-for-Wales Double Award specification](https://www.wjec.co.uk/media/1iyjmnoo/wjec-gcse-the-sciences-double-award-specification.pdf)\\n\\n- [WJEC GCSE Biology knowledge organisers](https://resources.wjec.co.uk/pages/ResourceSingle.aspx?rIid=3072)\\n\\n- [WJEC blended lessons and Biology revision support](https://resources-legacy.wjec.co.uk/Pages/ResourceByArgs?subId=3)\\n\\n- [WJEC past papers and marking schemes](https://www.wjec.co.uk/en/home/student-support/revision/past-papers/)\\n\\n- [WJEC Biology examiner report, summer 2026](https://www.wjec.co.uk/media/4fop54kq/wjec_gcse_biology_report__s26-e-1.pdf)\\n\\n- [WJEC Biology examiner report, summer 2025](https://www.wjec.co.uk/media/nczntene/wjec-gcse-biology-report-s25-e.pdf)\\n\\n- [WJEC Biology examiner report, summer 2024](https://www.wjec.co.uk/media/iyedbul2/wjec-gcse-biology-report-s24-e.pdf)\\n\\n- [Agarwal, Nunes & Blunt: classroom retrieval practice](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10648-021-09595-9)\\n\\n- [Cepeda et al.: review of distributed practice](https://www.yorku.ca/ncepeda/publications/CPVWR2006.html)\\n\\n- [Dunlosky et al.: effective learning techniques](https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/1529100612453266?icid=int.sj-full-text.citing-articles.331)","subject":"WJEC GCSE Biology","folder":"fieldbook-0","pinned":true,"website":{"url":"https://www.wjec.co.uk/media/m1lm4qb5/wjec-gcse-biology-from-2016-spec.pdf","pack":true},"topics":["fieldbook-topic-1-1","fieldbook-topic-1-2","fieldbook-topic-1-3","fieldbook-topic-1-4","fieldbook-topic-1-5","fieldbook-topic-1-6","fieldbook-topic-2-1","fieldbook-topic-2-2","fieldbook-topic-2-3","fieldbook-topic-2-4","fieldbook-topic-2-5","fieldbook-topic-2-6","fieldbook-topic-2-7","fieldbook-topic-2-8","fieldbook-topic-3"]},{"id":"fieldbook-topic-1-1","title":"1.1 Cells, membranes and enzymes","body":"## What to know\\n- Nucleus contains genetic material; cytoplasm is where many reactions occur; cell membrane controls exchange; mitochondria are the main site of aerobic respiration in these cells. Plant cells also have cellulose walls, a large vacuole and, in photosynthetic tissues, chloroplasts.\\n- A tissue is a group of cells working together; tissues form organs, and organs form systems. Link each adaptation to a function: root hair → larger surface area → more exchange; long neurone → transmits impulses over distance.\\n- Diffusion is net movement of particles down a concentration gradient. Osmosis is net movement of water through a partially permeable membrane from higher to lower water concentration. Active transport moves substances against their concentration gradient using energy from respiration.\\n- Enzymes are biological catalysts. Substrates fit an active site; sufficiently high temperatures or unsuitable pH can alter its shape. Low temperature generally slows collisions rather than denaturing an enzyme. Magnification = image size ÷ actual size; convert to the same units first.\\n\\n## Common mistake\\nOsmosis is about water, not sugar. Convert mm to µm before dividing.\\n\\n## Try it without notes\\nA potato cylinder loses mass in concentrated sugar solution. What moved out of its cells?\\n\\nA cell image is 36 mm long and the cell is 60 µm long. Calculate magnification and show working. [2]\\n\\n## Self-mark\\nWater by osmosis. The solution has a lower water concentration than the cell contents. Water moves out through partially permeable membranes.\\n\\n36 mm = 36,000 µm [1]; 36,000 ÷ 60 = ×600 [1]. Magnification has no units.\\n\\n## Read more\\n[WJEC separate GCSE Biology specification](https://www.wjec.co.uk/media/m1lm4qb5/wjec-gcse-biology-from-2016-spec.pdf) · [WJEC GCSE Biology knowledge organisers](https://resources.wjec.co.uk/pages/ResourceSingle.aspx?rIid=3072) · [WJEC Biology examiner report, summer 2026](https://www.wjec.co.uk/media/4fop54kq/wjec_gcse_biology_report__s26-e-1.pdf)","subject":"WJEC GCSE Biology","folder":"fieldbook-1","pinned":false,"study":{"question":"A potato cylinder loses mass in concentrated sugar solution. What moved out of its cells?","answer":"Water by osmosis.","explanation":"The solution has a lower water concentration than the cell contents. Water moves out through partially permeable membranes.","wrong":["Sugar by respiration.","Water by active transport.","Starch by diffusion."]},"written":[{"question":"A cell image is 36 mm long and the cell is 60 µm long. Calculate magnification and show working. [2]","answer":"36 mm = 36,000 µm [1]; 36,000 ÷ 60 = ×600 [1]. Magnification has no units."}]},{"id":"fieldbook-topic-1-2","title":"1.2 Respiration and breathing","body":"## What to know\\n- Aerobic respiration: glucose + oxygen → carbon dioxide + water; energy is transferred, with ATP available for cell processes. During intense exercise, anaerobic respiration in muscles transfers less energy per glucose and forms lactic acid.\\n- Air passes through the trachea, bronchi and bronchioles to alveoli. Mucus traps particles; cilia move mucus away from the lungs. Alveoli provide a large surface, short diffusion distance, and good blood supply for exchange.\\n- During inhalation the diaphragm contracts and flattens, and the rib cage moves up and out. Thorax volume increases, pressure falls below outside pressure, and air enters. Reverse the changes for quiet exhalation. A bell jar illustrates pressure and volume but has rigid sides, unlike the rib cage.\\n- Carbon monoxide reduces the oxygen-carrying capacity of blood; smoking damages cilia and is linked to emphysema and lung cancer. In emphysema, damaged alveolar walls reduce the area available for gas exchange.\\n\\n## Common mistake\\nBreathing moves air; respiration transfers energy in cells.\\n\\n## Try it without notes\\nWhat causes air to enter the lungs during inhalation?\\n\\nExplain why destruction of alveolar walls can make exercise harder. [3]\\n\\n## Self-mark\\nPressure in the lungs falls below atmospheric pressure. The chest cavity gets larger as the diaphragm contracts and flattens, lowering the pressure inside.\\n\\nSmaller surface area [1] reduces oxygen diffusion into the blood [1], limiting aerobic respiration in muscles [1].\\n\\n## Read more\\n[WJEC separate GCSE Biology specification](https://www.wjec.co.uk/media/m1lm4qb5/wjec-gcse-biology-from-2016-spec.pdf) · [WJEC GCSE Biology knowledge organisers](https://resources.wjec.co.uk/pages/ResourceSingle.aspx?rIid=3072) · [WJEC Biology examiner report, summer 2026](https://www.wjec.co.uk/media/4fop54kq/wjec_gcse_biology_report__s26-e-1.pdf)","subject":"WJEC GCSE Biology","folder":"fieldbook-1","pinned":false,"study":{"question":"What causes air to enter the lungs during inhalation?","answer":"Pressure in the lungs falls below atmospheric pressure.","explanation":"The chest cavity gets larger as the diaphragm contracts and flattens, lowering the pressure inside.","wrong":["Pressure in the lungs rises above atmospheric pressure.","The diaphragm relaxes and flattens.","The rib cage moves down and in."]},"written":[{"question":"Explain why destruction of alveolar walls can make exercise harder. [3]","answer":"Smaller surface area [1] reduces oxygen diffusion into the blood [1], limiting aerobic respiration in muscles [1]."}]},{"id":"fieldbook-topic-1-3","title":"1.3 Digestion and nutrition","body":"## What to know\\n- Digestion makes large, insoluble food molecules small enough to absorb. Carbohydrases break down carbohydrates, proteases break down proteins to amino acids, and lipases break down lipids to fatty acids and glycerol.\\n- The stomach churns food and provides acidic conditions for its protease. Peristalsis pushes food along. Bile is made in the liver, stored in the gall bladder, neutralises stomach acid entering the small intestine and emulsifies fat into smaller droplets, increasing area for lipase.\\n- Villi increase absorption area; a thin surface and blood supply help dissolved products enter circulation. Visking tubing models selective passage by size but lacks living cells, blood flow and active transport.\\n- Know positive food test results: iodine turns blue-black with starch; Benedict’s with heating changes towards orange/brick-red with reducing sugars; Biuret turns lilac with protein; the ethanol emulsion test gives a cloudy white emulsion with lipid. A balanced diet supplies energy and nutrients in appropriate amounts.\\n\\n## Common mistake\\nBile emulsifies fat; lipase digests it.\\n\\n## Try it without notes\\nHow does bile help lipase work faster?\\n\\nOnly glucose is detected outside a tube initially containing glucose and starch. Explain why. [2]\\n\\n## Self-mark\\nIt emulsifies fat, increasing the surface available to lipase. Breaking large droplets into smaller droplets increases their combined surface area. Bile itself is not an enzyme.\\n\\nSmall glucose molecules pass through the partially permeable tubing [1]; large starch molecules cannot [1].\\n\\n## Read more\\n[WJEC separate GCSE Biology specification](https://www.wjec.co.uk/media/m1lm4qb5/wjec-gcse-biology-from-2016-spec.pdf) · [WJEC GCSE Biology knowledge organisers](https://resources.wjec.co.uk/pages/ResourceSingle.aspx?rIid=3072) · [WJEC Biology examiner report, summer 2025](https://www.wjec.co.uk/media/nczntene/wjec-gcse-biology-report-s25-e.pdf)","subject":"WJEC GCSE Biology","folder":"fieldbook-1","pinned":false,"study":{"question":"How does bile help lipase work faster?","answer":"It emulsifies fat, increasing the surface available to lipase.","explanation":"Breaking large droplets into smaller droplets increases their combined surface area. Bile itself is not an enzyme.","wrong":["It is the enzyme that releases fatty acids.","It turns fat droplets into proteins.","It removes the need for a suitable pH."]},"written":[{"question":"Only glucose is detected outside a tube initially containing glucose and starch. Explain why. [2]","answer":"Small glucose molecules pass through the partially permeable tubing [1]; large starch molecules cannot [1]."}]},{"id":"fieldbook-topic-1-4","title":"1.4 Heart, blood and vessels","body":"## What to know\\n- Body → vena cava → right atrium → right ventricle → pulmonary artery → lungs → pulmonary vein → left atrium → left ventricle → aorta → body. Valves prevent backflow. Coronary arteries supply the heart muscle itself.\\n- Double circulation means blood passes through the heart twice per complete circuit: one circuit to lungs and another to the rest of the body. The left ventricle has thicker muscle to create pressure for the systemic circuit.\\n- Arteries carry blood away at relatively high pressure; veins return blood and often have valves. Capillaries have walls one cell thick for exchange. Red blood cells carry oxygen using haemoglobin, white blood cells defend, platelets help clotting, and plasma transports dissolved substances.\\n- Widening a narrowed coronary artery with angioplasty improves blood flow; statins can lower blood cholesterol. Compare benefits with limitations or risks; lifestyle changes can lower risk but may be difficult to sustain.\\n\\n## Common mistake\\nTrace blood from any starting point; do not confuse coronary and pulmonary vessels.\\n\\n## Try it without notes\\nWhich vessel carries oxygenated blood from the lungs to the heart?\\n\\nExplain how a narrowed coronary artery can reduce the ability of heart muscle to contract. [3]\\n\\n## Self-mark\\nPulmonary vein. The pulmonary vein returns blood from lungs to the left atrium. Artery and vein describe direction, not oxygen level.\\n\\nLess blood reaches the heart muscle [1], so less oxygen is available [1] for aerobic respiration and energy transfer [1].\\n\\n## Read more\\n[WJEC separate GCSE Biology specification](https://www.wjec.co.uk/media/m1lm4qb5/wjec-gcse-biology-from-2016-spec.pdf) · [WJEC GCSE Biology knowledge organisers](https://resources.wjec.co.uk/pages/ResourceSingle.aspx?rIid=3072) · [WJEC Biology examiner report, summer 2026](https://www.wjec.co.uk/media/4fop54kq/wjec_gcse_biology_report__s26-e-1.pdf)","subject":"WJEC GCSE Biology","folder":"fieldbook-1","pinned":false,"study":{"question":"Which vessel carries oxygenated blood from the lungs to the heart?","answer":"Pulmonary vein.","explanation":"The pulmonary vein returns blood from lungs to the left atrium. Artery and vein describe direction, not oxygen level.","wrong":["Pulmonary artery.","Vena cava.","Coronary artery."]},"written":[{"question":"Explain how a narrowed coronary artery can reduce the ability of heart muscle to contract. [3]","answer":"Less blood reaches the heart muscle [1], so less oxygen is available [1] for aerobic respiration and energy transfer [1]."}]},{"id":"fieldbook-topic-1-5","title":"1.5 Photosynthesis and plant transport","body":"## What to know\\n- Carbon dioxide + water → glucose + oxygen, using light absorbed by chlorophyll. Light, carbon dioxide or temperature can limit rate. If more light stops increasing rate, another factor may be limiting.\\n- Glucose can be respired, stored as starch, made into cellulose, or used with mineral nitrates to make amino acids. Palisade cells contain many chloroplasts; air spaces favour diffusion; guard cells control stomatal openings.\\n- Water evaporates from leaf cells and diffuses out through stomata. This loss draws water up the xylem, which also carries mineral ions. Phloem translocates dissolved sugars between sources and sinks. Wind may increase transpiration by removing moist air near a leaf.\\n- Nitrates support protein synthesis; magnesium is needed for chlorophyll. Know NPK nutrients and why deficiencies affect growth. A potometer estimates water uptake, not water loss directly; bubble distance alone is not volume unless you know tube dimensions.\\n\\n## Common mistake\\nA potometer estimates uptake; explain the mechanism behind each trend.\\n\\n## Try it without notes\\nWhat is the main substance moved from a source leaf to a growing sink in phloem?\\n\\nA bubble moves 36 mm in 90 seconds. Calculate its speed in mm/s and explain why this is not the exact transpiration rate. [3]\\n\\n## Self-mark\\nDissolved sugar. Phloem translocates dissolved sugars such as sucrose. Xylem transports water and mineral ions.\\n\\n36 ÷ 90 = 0.40 mm/s [1]; the bubble tracks water uptake [1], and some absorbed water may be used or retained rather than lost [1].\\n\\n## Read more\\n[WJEC separate GCSE Biology specification](https://www.wjec.co.uk/media/m1lm4qb5/wjec-gcse-biology-from-2016-spec.pdf) · [WJEC GCSE Biology knowledge organisers](https://resources.wjec.co.uk/pages/ResourceSingle.aspx?rIid=3072) · [WJEC Biology examiner report, summer 2026](https://www.wjec.co.uk/media/4fop54kq/wjec_gcse_biology_report__s26-e-1.pdf)","subject":"WJEC GCSE Biology","folder":"fieldbook-1","pinned":false,"study":{"question":"What is the main substance moved from a source leaf to a growing sink in phloem?","answer":"Dissolved sugar.","explanation":"Phloem translocates dissolved sugars such as sucrose. Xylem transports water and mineral ions.","wrong":["Oxygen gas.","Only water and mineral ions.","Insoluble starch grains."]},"written":[{"question":"A bubble moves 36 mm in 90 seconds. Calculate its speed in mm/s and explain why this is not the exact transpiration rate. [3]","answer":"36 ÷ 90 = 0.40 mm/s [1]; the bubble tracks water uptake [1], and some absorbed water may be used or retained rather than lost [1]."}]},{"id":"fieldbook-topic-1-6","title":"1.6 Ecosystems and nutrient cycles","body":"## What to know\\n- Food-chain arrows show energy transfer towards the consumer. Biomass transfer is inefficient: organisms respire and transfer energy to surroundings as heat, and not all material is eaten or digested. Pyramids of numbers and biomass measure different things.\\n- Carbon dioxide is removed from the air by photosynthesis. Carbon moves through feeding and returns through respiration, decomposition and combustion. In the nitrogen cycle distinguish nitrogen fixation, ammonification, nitrification and denitrification; trace where plants take up nitrates.\\n- Fertiliser run-off can cause an algal bloom; less light reaches aquatic plants and more material dies. Decomposer respiration consumes dissolved oxygen, which can kill aquatic animals. Sewage can also increase oxygen demand from decomposition.\\n- Persistent pollutants can accumulate in individuals and reach higher concentrations at higher trophic levels. Intensive farming can raise yields but reduce habitats or increase pollution. Use indicator species and measured data when evaluating water quality.\\n\\n## Common mistake\\nExplain oxygen loss through respiration, not just “less photosynthesis”.\\n\\n## Try it without notes\\nWhy can dissolved oxygen fall following fertiliser pollution?\\n\\nAn upstream site has 9 mg/L oxygen and a downstream site has 4 mg/L. Describe the difference and give one biologically plausible explanation. [3]\\n\\n## Self-mark\\nDecomposers respire as they break down dead material. Decomposition increases oxygen demand; simply saying the water is polluted does not explain the fall.\\n\\nDownstream is 5 mg/L lower, or explicitly compare 4 with 9 mg/L [1]. More organic material may be decomposing downstream [1], so respiration uses more dissolved oxygen [1].\\n\\n## Read more\\n[WJEC separate GCSE Biology specification](https://www.wjec.co.uk/media/m1lm4qb5/wjec-gcse-biology-from-2016-spec.pdf) · [WJEC GCSE Biology knowledge organisers](https://resources.wjec.co.uk/pages/ResourceSingle.aspx?rIid=3072) · [WJEC Biology examiner report, summer 2024](https://www.wjec.co.uk/media/iyedbul2/wjec-gcse-biology-report-s24-e.pdf)","subject":"WJEC GCSE Biology","folder":"fieldbook-1","pinned":false,"study":{"question":"Why can dissolved oxygen fall following fertiliser pollution?","answer":"Decomposers respire as they break down dead material.","explanation":"Decomposition increases oxygen demand; simply saying the water is polluted does not explain the fall.","wrong":["Fertiliser replaces oxygen with sunlight.","Algal growth stops all organisms respiring.","Fish use dissolved nitrates instead of oxygen."]},"written":[{"question":"An upstream site has 9 mg/L oxygen and a downstream site has 4 mg/L. Describe the difference and give one biologically plausible explanation. [3]","answer":"Downstream is 5 mg/L lower, or explicitly compare 4 with 9 mg/L [1]. More organic material may be decomposing downstream [1], so respiration uses more dissolved oxygen [1]."}]},{"id":"fieldbook-topic-2-1","title":"2.1 Classification and biodiversity","body":"## What to know\\n- Classification groups organisms using shared features and relationships. Genus and species together identify an organism by its binomial name. Distinguish main groups by their features rather than size or habitat alone.\\n- Biodiversity is the variety of living organisms. Within a species, intraspecific competition includes competition for mates; between species, interspecific competition may involve food or space. Behavioural and structural adaptations can improve survival.\\n- Use randomly placed quadrats to estimate abundance in a representative area; a transect helps examine changes along a gradient. Capture–recapture estimates population size = first catch × second catch ÷ marked recaptures. This assumes marks remain, individuals mix, and the population is reasonably stable.\\n- Evaluate conservation with competing costs and benefits. An introduced species can outcompete native species; biological control can reduce pests but may also have unintended effects on non-target species.\\n\\n## Common mistake\\nGive a specific sampling improvement, not just “make it fair”.\\n\\n## Try it without notes\\nWhat do randomly placed quadrats mainly reduce?\\n\\nIn a mark–recapture survey, 30 animals are marked, 40 caught later and 6 of the 40 have marks. Estimate the population and give one assumption. [3]\\n\\n## Self-mark\\nBias in deciding which areas to sample. Random sampling avoids deliberately selecting unusually dense or sparse areas. More well-distributed samples improve representativeness.\\n\\n30 × 40 ÷ 6 [1] = 200 [1]. For example, marked animals mix with the rest of the population before recapture [1].\\n\\n## Read more\\n[WJEC separate GCSE Biology specification](https://www.wjec.co.uk/media/m1lm4qb5/wjec-gcse-biology-from-2016-spec.pdf) · [WJEC GCSE Biology knowledge organisers](https://resources.wjec.co.uk/pages/ResourceSingle.aspx?rIid=3072) · [WJEC Biology examiner report, summer 2024](https://www.wjec.co.uk/media/iyedbul2/wjec-gcse-biology-report-s24-e.pdf)","subject":"WJEC GCSE Biology","folder":"fieldbook-2","pinned":false,"study":{"question":"What do randomly placed quadrats mainly reduce?","answer":"Bias in deciding which areas to sample.","explanation":"Random sampling avoids deliberately selecting unusually dense or sparse areas. More well-distributed samples improve representativeness.","wrong":["The area of the entire habitat.","The true population size.","The amount of variation in nature."]},"written":[{"question":"In a mark–recapture survey, 30 animals are marked, 40 caught later and 6 of the 40 have marks. Estimate the population and give one assumption. [3]","answer":"30 × 40 ÷ 6 [1] = 200 [1]. For example, marked animals mix with the rest of the population before recapture [1]."}]},{"id":"fieldbook-topic-2-2","title":"2.2 Mitosis, meiosis and stem cells","body":"## What to know\\n- Chromosomes contain DNA; genes are sections of DNA. Mitosis produces two genetically identical daughter cells with the same chromosome number. It supports growth, repair and asexual reproduction; uncontrolled mitosis can contribute to tumour formation.\\n- Meiosis produces gametes with half the chromosome number. The products are genetically varied. Fertilisation restores the full number when nuclei from two gametes fuse.\\n- Stem cells are unspecialised and can divide and differentiate. Embryonic stem cells can produce a wider range of specialised cells than most adult stem cells. Potential uses include replacing damaged tissue; considerations include rejection, safety and the ethics of embryo use.\\n\\n## Common mistake\\nSay “genetically identical”, not simply “identical”.\\n\\n## Try it without notes\\nWhat is the chromosome number of each normal human gamete?\\n\\nCompare mitosis and meiosis in terms of purpose, chromosome number and genetic similarity. [4]\\n\\n## Self-mark\\n23. Meiosis halves the chromosome number; fertilisation restores 46 in a normal human zygote.\\n\\nMitosis for growth or repair, meiosis for gamete formation [1]; same versus half chromosome number [1]; mitosis gives genetically identical cells [1], meiosis genetically varied cells [1].\\n\\n## Read more\\n[WJEC separate GCSE Biology specification](https://www.wjec.co.uk/media/m1lm4qb5/wjec-gcse-biology-from-2016-spec.pdf) · [WJEC GCSE Biology knowledge organisers](https://resources.wjec.co.uk/pages/ResourceSingle.aspx?rIid=3072) · [WJEC Biology examiner report, summer 2024](https://www.wjec.co.uk/media/iyedbul2/wjec-gcse-biology-report-s24-e.pdf)","subject":"WJEC GCSE Biology","folder":"fieldbook-2","pinned":false,"study":{"question":"What is the chromosome number of each normal human gamete?","answer":"23.","explanation":"Meiosis halves the chromosome number; fertilisation restores 46 in a normal human zygote.","wrong":["46.","92.","It varies with the age of the parent."]},"written":[{"question":"Compare mitosis and meiosis in terms of purpose, chromosome number and genetic similarity. [4]","answer":"Mitosis for growth or repair, meiosis for gamete formation [1]; same versus half chromosome number [1]; mitosis gives genetically identical cells [1], meiosis genetically varied cells [1]."}]},{"id":"fieldbook-topic-2-3","title":"2.3 DNA and inheritance","body":"## What to know\\n- DNA is a double helix with complementary base pairs A–T and C–G. A gene is a section of DNA; an allele is a version of a gene. A base triplet codes for an amino acid; an amino-acid sequence affects protein structure and function.\\n- Genotype is the allele combination; phenotype is the observable characteristic. For a simple dominant/recessive gene, two recessive alleles are usually needed for the recessive phenotype. A Punnett square gives probabilities, not guaranteed outcomes in a family.\\n- Polygenic traits are influenced by multiple genes and can also be affected by environment. In the typical human model, eggs carry X and sperm carry X or Y. X and Y are chromosomes, not dominant and recessive alleles.\\n- DNA profiles compare patterns to help assess identity or relatedness; consider uncertainty and privacy. Genetic modification can give an organism a new trait by transferring genetic information; weigh claimed benefits against environmental or ethical concerns.\\n\\n## Common mistake\\nAn allele is a version of a gene; a triplet codes for an amino acid.\\n\\n## Try it without notes\\nTwo carriers are Aa and Aa. What is the probability of a child with genotype aa?\\n\\nExplain how a change in a DNA base sequence might affect a protein, and why it might not. [3]\\n\\n## Self-mark\\n1 in 4. The outcomes are AA, Aa, Aa, aa. Each child has the same 1-in-4 probability under this model.\\n\\nA changed triplet may code for a different amino acid [1], which can change protein shape or function [1]. Some different triplets code for the same amino acid [1].\\n\\n## Read more\\n[WJEC separate GCSE Biology specification](https://www.wjec.co.uk/media/m1lm4qb5/wjec-gcse-biology-from-2016-spec.pdf) · [WJEC GCSE Biology knowledge organisers](https://resources.wjec.co.uk/pages/ResourceSingle.aspx?rIid=3072) · [WJEC Biology examiner report, summer 2026](https://www.wjec.co.uk/media/4fop54kq/wjec_gcse_biology_report__s26-e-1.pdf)","subject":"WJEC GCSE Biology","folder":"fieldbook-2","pinned":false,"study":{"question":"Two carriers are Aa and Aa. What is the probability of a child with genotype aa?","answer":"1 in 4.","explanation":"The outcomes are AA, Aa, Aa, aa. Each child has the same 1-in-4 probability under this model.","wrong":["1 in 2.","3 in 4.","0."]},"written":[{"question":"Explain how a change in a DNA base sequence might affect a protein, and why it might not. [3]","answer":"A changed triplet may code for a different amino acid [1], which can change protein shape or function [1]. Some different triplets code for the same amino acid [1]."}]},{"id":"fieldbook-topic-2-4","title":"2.4 Variation and evolution","body":"## What to know\\n- Genetic variation comes from different inherited information; environmental variation comes from conditions, and many traits have both causes. Height is continuous, while ABO blood group is discontinuous. A mutation is a change in DNA; its effect can be harmful, neutral or beneficial in context.\\n- Natural selection: individuals differ; a heritable trait helps some survive and reproduce in a particular environment; their alleles become more common over generations. Individuals do not evolve because they need to. Extinction can occur if populations cannot adapt or move in time.\\n- Sexual reproduction mixes inherited information; asexual reproduction usually makes clones, apart from mutations. Cystic fibrosis is commonly taught as recessive inheritance; use family-tree evidence and be clear about carriers and affected individuals.\\n- Resistance can increase when selection favours already resistant individuals. Genome information may help identify disease risk or guide treatments, but genetic risk is not certainty. Gene therapy aims to address a genetic cause; weigh potential benefit against limitations.\\n\\n## Common mistake\\nName the selection pressure in the question and finish with inheritance.\\n\\n## Try it without notes\\nAfter an environmental change, what changes during natural selection?\\n\\nOn dark soil, dark insects are less visible to predators. Explain how the population could change over generations. [4]\\n\\n## Self-mark\\nThe frequency of inherited variants in the population. Individuals with advantageous inherited variants tend to leave more offspring, so the variants can become more common over generations.\\n\\nInsects vary in colour [1]; dark ones are less likely to be eaten [1] and reproduce more successfully [1], so the inherited dark-colour variant becomes more common [1].\\n\\n## Read more\\n[WJEC separate GCSE Biology specification](https://www.wjec.co.uk/media/m1lm4qb5/wjec-gcse-biology-from-2016-spec.pdf) · [WJEC GCSE Biology knowledge organisers](https://resources.wjec.co.uk/pages/ResourceSingle.aspx?rIid=3072) · [WJEC Biology examiner report, summer 2026](https://www.wjec.co.uk/media/4fop54kq/wjec_gcse_biology_report__s26-e-1.pdf)","subject":"WJEC GCSE Biology","folder":"fieldbook-2","pinned":false,"study":{"question":"After an environmental change, what changes during natural selection?","answer":"The frequency of inherited variants in the population.","explanation":"Individuals with advantageous inherited variants tend to leave more offspring, so the variants can become more common over generations.","wrong":["All individuals acquire identical new alleles.","All adults change their genotype at the same time.","Organisms choose useful mutations."]},"written":[{"question":"On dark soil, dark insects are less visible to predators. Explain how the population could change over generations. [4]","answer":"Insects vary in colour [1]; dark ones are less likely to be eaten [1] and reproduce more successfully [1], so the inherited dark-colour variant becomes more common [1]."}]},{"id":"fieldbook-topic-2-5","title":"2.5 Response and regulation","body":"## What to know\\n- A reflex is rapid and automatic. Stimulus → receptor → sensory neurone → relay neurone in the CNS → motor neurone → effector → response. A synapse is a gap where chemical signals transfer the message forward.\\n- The iris controls pupil size, the lens focuses light, the retina contains receptors, and the optic nerve carries impulses. In bright light the pupil constricts; accommodation changes lens shape for near or distant vision.\\n- After blood glucose rises, insulin promotes uptake and storage as glycogen; when blood glucose falls, glucagon promotes conversion of liver glycogen back to glucose. Type 1 involves too little insulin; type 2 usually involves reduced responsiveness to insulin.\\n- When hot, skin arterioles dilate and more blood flows near the surface; evaporation of sweat transfers energy away. When cold, shivering raises heat production. Negative feedback opposes a change. In shoots, auxin can increase elongation on the shaded side, bending growth towards light.\\n\\n## Common mistake\\nBlood vessels dilate; they do not move towards the surface.\\n\\n## Try it without notes\\nWhich sequence is correct for a typical reflex arc?\\n\\nExplain two changes that increase heat loss from a runner on a hot day. [4]\\n\\n## Self-mark\\nSensory → relay → motor. The sensory neurone carries the signal into the CNS, a relay passes it on, and a motor neurone carries it to an effector.\\n\\nSkin arterioles dilate [1], increasing blood flow near the surface and heat loss [1]. Sweat production increases [1]; evaporation transfers energy from the skin [1].\\n\\n## Read more\\n[WJEC separate GCSE Biology specification](https://www.wjec.co.uk/media/m1lm4qb5/wjec-gcse-biology-from-2016-spec.pdf) · [WJEC GCSE Biology knowledge organisers](https://resources.wjec.co.uk/pages/ResourceSingle.aspx?rIid=3072) · [WJEC Biology examiner report, summer 2026](https://www.wjec.co.uk/media/4fop54kq/wjec_gcse_biology_report__s26-e-1.pdf)","subject":"WJEC GCSE Biology","folder":"fieldbook-2","pinned":false,"study":{"question":"Which sequence is correct for a typical reflex arc?","answer":"Sensory → relay → motor.","explanation":"The sensory neurone carries the signal into the CNS, a relay passes it on, and a motor neurone carries it to an effector.","wrong":["Motor → sensory → relay.","Relay → motor → sensory.","Sensory → motor → receptor."]},"written":[{"question":"Explain two changes that increase heat loss from a runner on a hot day. [4]","answer":"Skin arterioles dilate [1], increasing blood flow near the surface and heat loss [1]. Sweat production increases [1]; evaporation transfers energy from the skin [1]."}]},{"id":"fieldbook-topic-2-6","title":"2.6 Kidneys and water balance","body":"## What to know\\n- Kidneys remove urea and help control water and ions. Filtration in the glomerulus leaves blood cells and large proteins in the blood; selective reabsorption returns useful substances, including glucose, and varying amounts of water. Urine passes through ureters to the bladder and leaves by the urethra.\\n- When blood water content falls, more ADH is released. Collecting ducts become more permeable to water; more water is reabsorbed and the urine becomes more concentrated. The reverse follows when blood water content rises.\\n- In dialysis, small solutes can cross a partially permeable membrane. A low concentration of urea in dialysis fluid allows urea to leave blood by diffusion; countercurrent flow sustains the gradient. A transplant can reduce the need for regular dialysis but involves surgery, possible rejection and immunosuppression.\\n- Glucose or protein in urine can flag an issue and may need follow-up tests; do not diagnose from a single unexplained result.\\n\\n## Common mistake\\nSpecify water in the blood when explaining the feedback loop.\\n\\n## Try it without notes\\nHow does the body usually respond to dehydration?\\n\\nExplain why dialysis fluid is designed to contain a normal blood glucose concentration but little urea. [3]\\n\\n## Self-mark\\nMore ADH, more water reabsorbed, more concentrated urine. More ADH increases water permeability of collecting ducts; more water is reabsorbed into the blood.\\n\\nUrea has a concentration gradient [1] and diffuses out of the blood [1]. A similar glucose concentration avoids significant net loss of glucose [1].\\n\\n## Read more\\n[WJEC separate GCSE Biology specification](https://www.wjec.co.uk/media/m1lm4qb5/wjec-gcse-biology-from-2016-spec.pdf) · [WJEC GCSE Biology knowledge organisers](https://resources.wjec.co.uk/pages/ResourceSingle.aspx?rIid=3072) · [WJEC Biology examiner report, summer 2026](https://www.wjec.co.uk/media/4fop54kq/wjec_gcse_biology_report__s26-e-1.pdf)","subject":"WJEC GCSE Biology","folder":"fieldbook-2","pinned":false,"study":{"question":"How does the body usually respond to dehydration?","answer":"More ADH, more water reabsorbed, more concentrated urine.","explanation":"More ADH increases water permeability of collecting ducts; more water is reabsorbed into the blood.","wrong":["Less ADH, more water reabsorbed, more dilute urine.","More ADH, less water reabsorbed, more dilute urine.","No change in ADH, with more dilute urine."]},"written":[{"question":"Explain why dialysis fluid is designed to contain a normal blood glucose concentration but little urea. [3]","answer":"Urea has a concentration gradient [1] and diffuses out of the blood [1]. A similar glucose concentration avoids significant net loss of glucose [1]."}]},{"id":"fieldbook-topic-2-7","title":"2.7 Micro-organisms and applications","body":"## What to know\\n- Micro-organisms include bacteria and microscopic fungi. A visible colony is a population, not one cell; under idealised counting assumptions, one viable starting unit gives one colony, but a starting clump can also give one.\\n- Refrigeration generally slows bacterial growth; it does not sterilise food. Asepsis means preventing unwanted contamination. Distinguish contamination control from interpreting evidence in an already collected data set.\\n- Penicillium is a fungus associated with the discovery and production of penicillin. A fermenter is a controlled industrial vessel. Learn to interpret the purpose of control and distinguish the organism from the useful product.\\n\\n## Common mistake\\nOne colony is a population, and freezing does not guarantee all bacteria are dead.\\n\\n## Try it without notes\\nWhy does refrigeration help food last longer?\\n\\nTwo ready-made data sets for food samples show 12 and 72 viable counts after storage. Give a comparison and one reason not to conclude that storage alone caused the difference. [2]\\n\\n## Self-mark\\nIt generally slows microbial growth. Cooling generally slows processes involved in growth and reproduction; it does not make food sterile.\\n\\nThe second count is six times the first, or 60 higher [1]. Another variable could differ, such as the starting count [1].\\n\\n## Read more\\n[WJEC separate GCSE Biology specification](https://www.wjec.co.uk/media/m1lm4qb5/wjec-gcse-biology-from-2016-spec.pdf) · [WJEC GCSE Biology knowledge organisers](https://resources.wjec.co.uk/pages/ResourceSingle.aspx?rIid=3072) · [WJEC Biology examiner report, summer 2026](https://www.wjec.co.uk/media/4fop54kq/wjec_gcse_biology_report__s26-e-1.pdf)","subject":"WJEC GCSE Biology","folder":"fieldbook-2","pinned":false,"study":{"question":"Why does refrigeration help food last longer?","answer":"It generally slows microbial growth.","explanation":"Cooling generally slows processes involved in growth and reproduction; it does not make food sterile.","wrong":["It makes the food permanently sterile.","It kills all bacteria immediately.","It removes all nutrients from the food."]},"written":[{"question":"Two ready-made data sets for food samples show 12 and 72 viable counts after storage. Give a comparison and one reason not to conclude that storage alone caused the difference. [2]","answer":"The second count is six times the first, or 60 higher [1]. Another variable could differ, such as the starting count [1]."}]},{"id":"fieldbook-topic-2-8","title":"2.8 Disease, defence and treatment","body":"## What to know\\n- Communicable diseases can spread. HIV is caused by a virus, chlamydia by bacteria, and malaria by Plasmodium carried by mosquitoes. Distinguish the pathogen from its vector and link prevention to the transmission route.\\n- Skin and mucus are barriers; phagocytes engulf pathogens; lymphocytes produce specific antibodies. An antigen triggers a specific immune response. Antitoxins neutralise toxins. Vaccination prepares immune memory so a future response is faster.\\n- Antibiotics act against susceptible bacteria, not viruses. Antibiotic use can select for resistant bacteria; MRSA is an example. Monoclonal antibodies recognise one target antigen and can be used in tests or targeted treatment.\\n- Drug development examines safety, dose and efficacy. Randomisation reduces allocation bias; placebo provides a comparison; double-blinding means participants and relevant researchers do not know assignments during the trial. Distinguish treatment effect from expectation or observer bias.\\n\\n## Common mistake\\nThe lymphocyte makes antibodies; the antigen triggers the response.\\n\\n## Try it without notes\\nWhat does double-blind mean in an appropriate drug trial?\\n\\nExplain why a vaccine can protect someone without directly killing a pathogen. [3]\\n\\n## Self-mark\\nNeither participants nor relevant researchers know who receives which treatment during the trial. Double-blinding reduces expectation and observer bias. A placebo is a comparison treatment, not a type of blinding.\\n\\nVaccine antigens stimulate a specific immune response [1]. Memory cells remain [1] and enable a faster response on later exposure [1].\\n\\n## Read more\\n[WJEC separate GCSE Biology specification](https://www.wjec.co.uk/media/m1lm4qb5/wjec-gcse-biology-from-2016-spec.pdf) · [WJEC GCSE Biology knowledge organisers](https://resources.wjec.co.uk/pages/ResourceSingle.aspx?rIid=3072) · [WJEC Biology examiner report, summer 2024](https://www.wjec.co.uk/media/iyedbul2/wjec-gcse-biology-report-s24-e.pdf)","subject":"WJEC GCSE Biology","folder":"fieldbook-2","pinned":false,"study":{"question":"What does double-blind mean in an appropriate drug trial?","answer":"Neither participants nor relevant researchers know who receives which treatment during the trial.","explanation":"Double-blinding reduces expectation and observer bias. A placebo is a comparison treatment, not a type of blinding.","wrong":["Every participant receives a placebo twice.","Each participant receives two doses.","Trial results are never released."]},"written":[{"question":"Explain why a vaccine can protect someone without directly killing a pathogen. [3]","answer":"Vaccine antigens stimulate a specific immune response [1]. Memory cells remain [1] and enable a faster response on later exposure [1]."}]},{"id":"fieldbook-topic-3","title":"3 Scientific enquiry and data skills","body":"## What to know\\n- Confirm your cohort with school. WJEC says the January–February 2027 sitting uses the legacy Unit 3; the revised assessment is for Year 10 starters from September 2026 and is first awarded in 2028. In the revised format, Unit 3 is untiered, 10%, 28 marks: one chosen enquiry, a one-hour practical task (6), and a one-hour written task (22).\\n- A strong hypothesis states the direction of a change in the dependent variable as the independent variable changes. Name measured quantities precisely. State a genuine control variable and how you keep it constant, not just “keep it fair”.\\n- Put the independent variable on x and the dependent on y unless directed otherwise, label both with units, use equal scale steps and follow the line instructions. Rate = change ÷ time; percentage change = (new − original) ÷ original × 100. Show substitutions and round only at the end.\\n- Repeatability compares repetitions under the same conditions; reproducibility checks whether a similar result is obtained under changed conditions, for example by another group. Accuracy is closeness to the true value. Quote the data when judging confidence, explain anomalies before excluding, and match each improvement to an identified weakness.\\n- Specified-practical checklist: cell microscopy; enzyme activity; food energy; photosynthesis; transpiration; species distribution and abundance; variation; reaction time; artificial urine tests. For each, practise identifying the outcome and the variables, drawing a graph, and evaluating a conclusion. Confirm your centre’s assessment instructions.\\n\\n## Common mistake\\nRepeating can improve confidence in a mean; it does not fix a systematic error.\\n\\n## Try it without notes\\nThree times measured by the same group are 19.1, 19.2 and 19.0 s. Which conclusion is justified?\\n\\nAt a low light setting an aquatic plant releases 6, 7 and 5 bubbles/min; at a high setting it releases 12, 10 and 11. Compare the means, then suggest one measurement improvement and explain why. [4]\\n\\n## Self-mark\\nThe times are close together, suggesting repeatability; accuracy is not established. The range is 0.2 s. Similar repeats indicate repeatability; without a reference value or another group, neither accuracy nor reproducibility has been established.\\n\\nLow mean 6 and high mean 11 bubbles/min [2]; collect or measure gas volume per minute [1], because individual bubbles may differ in size [1].\\n\\n## Read more\\n[WJEC separate GCSE Biology specification](https://www.wjec.co.uk/media/m1lm4qb5/wjec-gcse-biology-from-2016-spec.pdf) · [WJEC GCSE Biology knowledge organisers](https://resources.wjec.co.uk/pages/ResourceSingle.aspx?rIid=3072) · [WJEC Biology examiner report, summer 2026](https://www.wjec.co.uk/media/4fop54kq/wjec_gcse_biology_report__s26-e-1.pdf)","subject":"WJEC GCSE Biology","folder":"fieldbook-3","pinned":false,"study":{"question":"Three times measured by the same group are 19.1, 19.2 and 19.0 s. Which conclusion is justified?","answer":"The times are close together, suggesting repeatability; accuracy is not established.","explanation":"The range is 0.2 s. Similar repeats indicate repeatability; without a reference value or another group, neither accuracy nor reproducibility has been established.","wrong":["The close times prove the instrument is calibrated.","The times prove that other groups will get the same results.","The largest time must be an anomaly."]},"written":[{"question":"At a low light setting an aquatic plant releases 6, 7 and 5 bubbles/min; at a high setting it releases 12, 10 and 11. Compare the means, then suggest one measurement improvement and explain why. [4]","answer":"Low mean 6 and high mean 11 bubbles/min [2]; collect or measure gas volume per minute [1], because individual bubbles may differ in size [1]."}]},{"id":"fieldbook-board-1","title":"Biology spider map: Unit 1","body":"Pick any topic card to open the corresponding revision note.","subject":"WJEC GCSE Biology","folder":"fieldbook-1","pinned":false,"board":{"layout":"spider","hub":{"x":580,"y":360},"video":null,"nodes":[{"id":"fieldbook-topic-1-1","note":"fieldbook-topic-1-1","title":"1.1 Cells, membranes and enzymes","text":"Osmosis is about water, not sugar. Convert mm to µm before dividing.","color":"sage","x":122,"y":360},{"id":"fieldbook-topic-1-2","note":"fieldbook-topic-1-2","title":"1.2 Respiration and breathing","text":"Breathing moves air; respiration transfers energy in cells.","color":"sage","x":1038,"y":360},{"id":"fieldbook-topic-1-3","note":"fieldbook-topic-1-3","title":"1.3 Digestion and nutrition","text":"Bile emulsifies fat; lipase digests it.","color":"sage","x":580,"y":81},{"id":"fieldbook-topic-1-4","note":"fieldbook-topic-1-4","title":"1.4 Heart, blood and vessels","text":"Trace blood from any starting point; do not confuse coronary and pulmonary vessels.","color":"sage","x":580,"y":639},{"id":"fieldbook-topic-1-5","note":"fieldbook-topic-1-5","title":"1.5 Photosynthesis and plant transport","text":"A potometer estimates uptake; explain the mechanism behind each trend.","color":"sage","x":122,"y":208},{"id":"fieldbook-topic-1-6","note":"fieldbook-topic-1-6","title":"1.6 Ecosystems and nutrient cycles","text":"Explain oxygen loss through respiration, not just “less photosynthesis”.","color":"sage","x":1038,"y":208},{"id":"other-board","note":"fieldbook-board-2","title":"Biology spider map: Unit 2","text":"Open the other topic map.","color":"ink","x":122,"y":512}],"edges":[["centre","fieldbook-topic-1-1"],["centre","fieldbook-topic-1-2"],["centre","fieldbook-topic-1-3"],["centre","fieldbook-topic-1-4"],["centre","fieldbook-topic-1-5"],["centre","fieldbook-topic-1-6"],["centre","other-board"]]}},{"id":"fieldbook-board-2","title":"Biology spider map: Unit 2","body":"Pick any topic card to open the corresponding revision note.","subject":"WJEC GCSE Biology","folder":"fieldbook-2","pinned":false,"board":{"layout":"spider","hub":{"x":580,"y":360},"video":null,"nodes":[{"id":"fieldbook-topic-2-1","note":"fieldbook-topic-2-1","title":"2.1 Classification and biodiversity","text":"Give a specific sampling improvement, not just “make it fair”.","color":"ochre","x":122,"y":360},{"id":"fieldbook-topic-2-2","note":"fieldbook-topic-2-2","title":"2.2 Mitosis, meiosis and stem cells","text":"Say “genetically identical”, not simply “identical”.","color":"ochre","x":1038,"y":360},{"id":"fieldbook-topic-2-3","note":"fieldbook-topic-2-3","title":"2.3 DNA and inheritance","text":"An allele is a version of a gene; a triplet codes for an amino acid.","color":"ochre","x":580,"y":81},{"id":"fieldbook-topic-2-4","note":"fieldbook-topic-2-4","title":"2.4 Variation and evolution","text":"Name the selection pressure in the question and finish with inheritance.","color":"ochre","x":580,"y":639},{"id":"fieldbook-topic-2-5","note":"fieldbook-topic-2-5","title":"2.5 Response and regulation","text":"Blood vessels dilate; they do not move towards the surface.","color":"ochre","x":122,"y":208},{"id":"fieldbook-topic-2-6","note":"fieldbook-topic-2-6","title":"2.6 Kidneys and water balance","text":"Specify water in the blood when explaining the feedback loop.","color":"ochre","x":1038,"y":208},{"id":"fieldbook-topic-2-7","note":"fieldbook-topic-2-7","title":"2.7 Micro-organisms and applications","text":"One colony is a population, and freezing does not guarantee all bacteria are dead.","color":"ochre","x":122,"y":512},{"id":"fieldbook-topic-2-8","note":"fieldbook-topic-2-8","title":"2.8 Disease, defence and treatment","text":"The lymphocyte makes antibodies; the antigen triggers the response.","color":"ochre","x":1038,"y":512},{"id":"other-board","note":"fieldbook-board-1","title":"Biology spider map: Unit 1","text":"Open the other topic map.","color":"ink","x":343,"y":81}],"edges":[["centre","fieldbook-topic-2-1"],["centre","fieldbook-topic-2-2"],["centre","fieldbook-topic-2-3"],["centre","fieldbook-topic-2-4"],["centre","fieldbook-topic-2-5"],["centre","fieldbook-topic-2-6"],["centre","fieldbook-topic-2-7"],["centre","fieldbook-topic-2-8"],["centre","other-board"]]}},{"id":"fieldbook-mock","title":"Mixed mini-paper · 26 marks","body":"Try this before looking at the answers. 26 marks · 34 minutes suggested. This is original practice, not an official WJEC paper or grade predictor.\\n\\n### 1. A root cell takes up nitrate ions when their concentration is lower in the surrounding soil than inside the cell. Name and explain the process. [3]\\n\\n### 2. A river’s oxygen reading falls from 8.0 to 5.2 mg/L downstream of a discharge. Calculate the percentage decrease and give one plausible mechanism. [4]\\n\\n### 3. Explain why higher carbon dioxide might increase photosynthesis in bright light but make little difference in dim light. [3]\\n\\n### 4. A person drinks very little on a hot day. Explain how ADH helps restore water balance. [4]\\n\\n### 5. A recessive disorder is represented by allele d. Neither parent is affected but their child has the condition. Give the parental genotypes and explain the chance for their next child. [3]\\n\\n### 6. A pulse sensor consistently reads 5 beats/min above a reference device. A class repeats its measurements five times. Explain what repeating can and cannot improve. [3]\\n\\n### 7. Planning: A student will investigate the effect of lamp distance on photosynthesis in an aquatic plant. State the independent and dependent variables, a suitable control and how to keep it constant, a directional hypothesis, and a way to collect more convincing data. [6]\\n\\n[[Mixed mini-paper · Self-marking guide]]","subject":"WJEC GCSE Biology","folder":"fieldbook-3","pinned":false},{"id":"fieldbook-marks","title":"Mixed mini-paper · Self-marking guide","body":"Award one mark per labelled point, except where a two-mark block is shown. Accept equivalent correct wording. Give your misses a reason: recall, applying knowledge, or interpreting data.\\n\\n### 1. A root cell takes up nitrate ions when their concentration is lower in the surrounding soil than inside the cell. Name and explain the process. [3]\\n\\nActive transport [1]. Ions move from lower to higher concentration [1], using energy from respiration [1].\\n\\n### 2. A river’s oxygen reading falls from 8.0 to 5.2 mg/L downstream of a discharge. Calculate the percentage decrease and give one plausible mechanism. [4]\\n\\nDifference 2.8 mg/L [1]; 2.8 ÷ 8.0 × 100 = 35% [1]. Organic matter supports decomposition [1]; decomposer respiration consumes dissolved oxygen [1].\\n\\n### 3. Explain why higher carbon dioxide might increase photosynthesis in bright light but make little difference in dim light. [3]\\n\\nCO₂ is a reactant [1]. It can limit the rate in bright light [1]; in dim light the light supply may instead limit the rate [1].\\n\\n### 4. A person drinks very little on a hot day. Explain how ADH helps restore water balance. [4]\\n\\nBlood water content falls [1], so more ADH is released [1]. Collecting ducts become more permeable [1]; more water moves back into the blood [1].\\n\\n### 5. A recessive disorder is represented by allele d. Neither parent is affected but their child has the condition. Give the parental genotypes and explain the chance for their next child. [3]\\n\\nBoth are Dd [1]; each can pass on d [1], giving 1 in 4 (25%) dd for the next child [1].\\n\\n### 6. A pulse sensor consistently reads 5 beats/min above a reference device. A class repeats its measurements five times. Explain what repeating can and cannot improve. [3]\\n\\nRepeats let the class assess repeatability or reduce the effect of random variation in a mean [1]. The systematic +5 bias remains [1]. A calibration check or correction is needed for accuracy [1].\\n\\n### 7. Planning: A student will investigate the effect of lamp distance on photosynthesis in an aquatic plant. State the independent and dependent variables, a suitable control and how to keep it constant, a directional hypothesis, and a way to collect more convincing data. [6]\\n\\nLamp distance [1]; gas volume per unit time or another defensible rate measure [1]; a relevant control and method, e.g. monitor and maintain water temperature in a water bath [2]; predict lower rate at longer distance [1]; repeat at each distance and compare means [1]. This is an original six-point drill, not an official level-of-response mark scheme.\\n\\n[[Mixed mini-paper · 26 marks]]","subject":"WJEC GCSE Biology","folder":"fieldbook-3","pinned":false},{"id":"fieldbook-exam","title":"Examiner lessons and revision plan","body":"Separate WJEC GCSE Biology (3400QS / C00/0779/8), not Eduqas or either WJEC Double Award. Units 1 and 2 each have an 80-mark, 1 h 45 min paper worth 45%. The specification uses bold for Higher-only content; check your tier against it.\\n\\nFor students sitting Unit 3 in January–February 2027, WJEC lists the legacy Practical Assessment. For Year 10 starters from September 2026, WJEC specifies revised Scientific Enquiry, first awarded in 2028. Ask your teacher which applies if your entry is unusual. The earlier examiner reports describe the legacy assessment, not the new format.\\n\\n## Where marks go\\n\\n- **Make comparisons explicit:** Write “4 mg/L lower downstream” rather than copying two numbers without saying what they show. A trend is change across an interval, not a comparison at a single point. ([WJEC Biology examiner report, summer 2024](https://www.wjec.co.uk/media/iyedbul2/wjec-gcse-biology-report-s24-e.pdf))\\n\\n- **Use the data and the biology:** When asked to explain, connect the observation to a mechanism. Example: as temperature rises beyond the optimum, the active site changes shape so less substrate binds. ([WJEC Biology examiner report, summer 2025](https://www.wjec.co.uk/media/nczntene/wjec-gcse-biology-report-s25-e.pdf))\\n\\n- **Name the variable and its measurement:** “Water volume, kept at 50 mL using a measuring cylinder” is more useful than “same amount of water”. Name the apparatus or value only where the question warrants it. ([WJEC Biology examiner report, summer 2026](https://www.wjec.co.uk/media/4fop54kq/wjec_gcse_biology_report__s26-e-1.pdf))\\n\\n- **Check which kind of confidence you mean:** Close repeats show repeatability, not accuracy; a consistent offset can remain. Compare groups explicitly when evaluating reproducibility and state a sensible improvement for the error you identified. ([WJEC Biology examiner report, summer 2026](https://www.wjec.co.uk/media/4fop54kq/wjec_gcse_biology_report__s26-e-1.pdf))\\n\\n- **Build longer answers around the command word:** For compare, say how the two things differ. For explain, give a causal chain. For evaluate, use evidence for and against and finish with a justified conclusion. ([WJEC Biology examiner report, summer 2024](https://www.wjec.co.uk/media/iyedbul2/wjec-gcse-biology-report-s24-e.pdf))\\n\\n## A repeatable study routine\\n\\nTry each question closed-book, check the explanation, then jot one mistake and redo it on another day. As a starting plan, revisit after 1, 3 and 7 days; shorten the gap for topics you still miss. ([Agarwal, Nunes & Blunt: classroom retrieval practice](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10648-021-09595-9); [Cepeda et al.: review of distributed practice](https://www.yorku.ca/ncepeda/publications/CPVWR2006.html); [Dunlosky et al.: effective learning techniques](https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/1529100612453266?icid=int.sj-full-text.citing-articles.331))\\n\\n## What to do next\\n\\nTry the mini-paper, then attempt a matching WJEC past paper for your unit and tier. Use the official mark scheme rather than converting your mini-paper score into a grade. [WJEC past papers and marking schemes](https://www.wjec.co.uk/en/home/student-support/revision/past-papers/)","subject":"WJEC GCSE Biology","folder":"fieldbook-3","pinned":false}]'),a={kind:e,version:1,folders:o,subjects:i,notes:n};export{a as default,o as folders,e as kind,n as notes,i as subjects,t as version};
